  "body": "## What is broken\n\n`terraform init` fails at the repository root. It has been failing for anyone\nwho tried to run Terraform here:\n\n```\n- Finding hashicorp/google versions matching \"~> 5.0, ~> 7.43\"...\n\nError: Failed to query available provider packages\n\nCould not retrieve the list of available versions for provider\nhashicorp/google: no available releases match the given constraints\n~> 5.0, ~> 7.43\n```\n\nTerraform intersects the root module's provider constraint with the constraint\ndeclared by every module the root calls. Twenty `.tf` files pin `~> 5.0`.\nExactly one, `terraform/modules/gke-cluster/main.tf`, pins `~> 7.43`. Those\nranges do not overlap, so no published provider satisfies both and `init` stops\nbefore it can do anything else.\n\nTwo roots call that module and both were blocked:\n\n| Root | Before | After |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `terraform/` | `init` fails | `init` + `validate` pass |\n| `clusters/prod-checkout-gateway-11/terraform/` | `init` fails | `init` + `validate` pass |\n\nThe other 17 roots inline their own cluster definitions, never call the module,\nand were unaffected.\n\n## The fix\n\nA child module should declare the oldest provider it is known to work against\nand leave the upper bound to the root module, which is the configuration that\nowns the lockfile and the state. Pinning a range inside a shared module makes\nthat module uncombinable with any root outside the range.\n\n```diff\n   google = {\n     source  = \"hashicorp/google\"\n-    version = \"~> 7.43\"\n+    version = \">= 5.0\"\n   }\n```\n\n`terraform/modules/gce-instance` gets the same treatment. It was not failing,\nbecause its `~> 5.0` happens to match the roots, but it is the identical latent\nfault and would reproduce this outage the first time any root moved to 6.x.\n\n## The bug this was hiding\n\nOnce `init` succeeds, `clusters/prod-checkout-gateway-11` fails `validate` for\nan unrelated reason:\n\n```\nError: Reference to undeclared resource\n\n  on outputs.tf line 2, in output \"cluster_name\":\n   2:   value = google_container_cluster.primary.name\n\nA managed resource \"google_container_cluster\" \"primary\" has not been\ndeclared in the root module.\n```\n\nThis cluster is the only one of the seventeen that has been migrated to call\nthe shared module. The migration moved the resources into the module but left\n`outputs.tf` referencing them as though they were still declared in the root.\nNobody had seen this because `validate` cannot run until `init` succeeds, and\n`init` never did. Fixing the constraint is what made the error reachable.\n\nThe module already exports the three values, so they map across one to one and\nkeep the same names and values for consumers:\n\n```diff\n output \"cluster_name\" {\n-  value = google_container_cluster.primary.name\n+  value = module.gke_cluster.cluster_name\n }\n```\n\n## Why not upgrade the fleet to 7.x instead\n\nThat also works. I tested it: setting every root to `~> 7.43` resolves\n`hashicorp/google v7.46.1` and validates with zero errors.\n\nIt is the wrong change for this pull request. It crosses two major provider\nversions, each with its own breaking changes, and the only way to know it is\nsafe is to run `terraform plan` against real state and read the diff. That is a\nmigration with a rollback story, not a bug fix. This pull request restores the\nrepository to a buildable state without changing which provider anyone gets;\n`>= 5.0` still resolves to `v5.45.2` today. The 6.x/7.x upgrade should be its\nown change, and the constraint layout in this pull request is the thing that\nmakes it possible to do one root at a time.\n\n## Verification\n\nTerraform 1.7.5, run against this branch:\n\n```\nterraform fmt -check -recursive     PASS\nroots checked: 19, failed: 0\nprovider resolved: hashicorp/google v5.45.2\n```\n\nRun against `main`, the same sweep fails at the `terraform/` root.\n\n## Scope notes for the reviewer\n\nThree separable commits:\n\n1. `822033c` — the constraint fix. This is the one that unblocks `init`. The\n   node pool whitespace in it is what `terraform fmt` produces; the file was\n   already unformatted before this change.\n2. `dabd796` — the `outputs.tf` fix. Only reachable because of commit 1.\n3. `341279b` — a CI workflow running `fmt`, `init -backend=false` and\n   `validate` across all 19 roots. Both faults here were static errors\n   detectable with no credentials and no state, and nothing caught them because\n   the repository has no workflows at all, despite `README.md` describing a\n   GitHub Actions pipeline. Drop this commit if you would rather land CI\n   separately; commits 1 and 2 stand without it.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **DO NOT MERGE.** This branch is referenced as a worked example in an\n> in-flight cost analysis and needs to stay open and unmerged.\n",
